A||N||11||hoppe||Newbie||||1||
Z||000000||hoppe||09-23-2000||11:20 PM||||I am new to Fiero's and new to the forum. Just bought an 88 GT T-top and need to find some parts. <BR>Can anyone suggest a source for brake rotors, suspension bushing and T-top weatherstripping?<P>It is a clean car but owned by a gal who never thought about getting keeping the suspension tuned since she bought it in 88.<P>Also, any tips or general advice appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||2xsess||09-24-2000||12:20 AM||||Welcome! Congrats on your new car. Good find especially w/T-tops.<P>There are alot of places that offer Fiero parts, it's just a matter of if you want to replace them with stock or aftermarket.<BR>There are also a lot of the forum members who can help you out. I don't have all their websites. Here are a few to get you started; <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <BR>Also the Fiero Shop in CA but I'm not sure of their site address.<BR>I've heard quite a few of members talking about PST for suspension bushings.<P>Well I hope that will help get you started. I'm sure the other members have alot of input for you too.<P>Good Luck!<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-24-2000||12:37 AM||||Many parts are available thru your local parts house.<P>this place has best price on pollyurethane. <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/" TARGET=_blank>Suspension Restoration Parts Co.</A> I would at the least replace the cradle bushings with polly or AL. (I recomend starting there then see what else the suspension needs.) Someone around the forum is making AL ones for about $100.<P>I just put polly in the cradle on mine. made a huge diferance. The rest will be replaced with OEM rubber type bushings. I may do the rear control arms in polly. I haven't decided on that yet. Polly in areas with allot of movement can be a real pain.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever2||09-24-2000||01:39 AM||||Welcome... you'll find more info here than you'll be able to retain.  And it won't take you long to find lots of interesting websites.  A quick diversion from the topic: what part of TX are you at (bad grammer I know)?<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000004||Standard||09-24-2000||03:49 AM||||The '88 cradles don't use any bushings, they're bolted directly to the frame  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000005||hoppe||09-24-2000||10:14 AM||||Cheever2, WE are located in Columbus, Texas. It is about 60 miles west of Houston.<P>I drove the car here from L.A. last weekend and had no problems except that I ran out of gas in the desert east of Blythe just before dark. The gas gage reads high- like bone dry at 1/4.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||RossT||09-24-2000||10:27 AM||||T-top parts are hard to come by.  Tim at <A HREF="http://www.fieroparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroparts.com</A>  bought the remaining supply of c&c fiero t-top parts. Keep checking the Mall also.  Mr fiero hangs out there and can normally find you good used parts.||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FieroGTRacingInc||09-24-2000||12:26 PM||||Glad to have more Texan's in the Forum..||||reg||1
